# _redirects — Cloudflare Pages. Path-level rules only.
#
# The apex → www redirect is NOT here and cannot be. `_redirects` is
# matched per path, after Cloudflare has already resolved the hostname,
# so it cannot see which host the request arrived on. divyakush.com →
# www.divyakush.com is a zone-level Redirect Rule in the Cloudflare
# dashboard; it is configured and currently returns 301 (verified:
# http://divyakush.com → https://divyakush.com → https://www.divyakush.com).
# Changing it means changing it there, not in this file.
#
# ── Why there are no path rules either ────────────────────────────
#
# This file used to carry `/*  /index.html  200` — the standard SPA
# fallback for a client-rendered site with one document. It cannot come
# back, for a reason that outlived the framework it was written against.
#
# The build writes a real document per route: out/projects/netra.html,
# out/insights/devjams.html, and twenty-one more, each with its own
# title, description, canonical, Open Graph and JSON-LD. Cloudflare's
# own documentation says redirects "are always followed, regardless of
# whether or not an asset matches the incoming request" — so a catch-all
# would shadow every one of those files and serve the homepage's title
# and canonical for all of them. That is the exact bug the per-route
# documents exist to fix, and it would be invisible in a browser.
#
# Nothing is lost by leaving it empty. Pages serves <path>.html for
# <path> on its own, so every valid route answers. Unknown paths fall to
# out/404.html and return a real 404 status rather than 200-with-a-
# 404-page, which is a soft 404 and something Google reports as an
# error.
#
# scripts/serve-dist.mjs reproduces both behaviours locally, and
# scripts/audit-site.mjs fails if either stops holding.
#
# ── The one path rule ─────────────────────────────────────────
#
# /projects was a 404. It is the URL a person types when they want the
# work, and the one an outside link guesses at, so it should answer.
#
# It resolves to the work section of the home page rather than to a new
# index page, because that section already IS the projects index — all
# ten of them, with covers, in the deck. A second page listing the same
# ten would be a duplicate of a section that already ranks.
#
# 301 and not 302: this is permanent. The consequence is that Google
# folds /projects into / and never indexes it separately, which is the
# correct outcome — it is an alias, not a page. It is deliberately NOT
# in sitemap.xml for the same reason: a sitemap lists canonical
# destinations, and submitting a redirect is a Search Console error.
#
# The fragment survives the hop. A browser applies it after following
# the Location header, so the visitor lands on the work section.

/projects  /#work  301

# ── The Search Console verification file ──────────────────────────
#
# Google's HTML-file verification requires that the exact URL
#
#     /google0dbb222546d95f4f.html
#
# answers 200 with the token in it. Cloudflare Pages will not do that
# on its own: it normalises every `.html` request to the extensionless
# path with a 308, so the file that was uploaded to prove ownership of
# the domain answers a redirect instead of the token. Verified with
# curl — `HTTP/1.1 308`, `Location: /google0dbb222546d95f4f`.
#
# Nothing is currently broken by that. The live property is verified by
# DNS TXT on the apex, which is strictly better: a Domain property
# covers every subdomain and both protocols, where an HTML file only
# ever proves one host. That is why this went unnoticed.
#
# It is fixed anyway, because the failure mode is nasty and delayed. If
# the DNS record is ever dropped — a nameserver move, a zone rebuild —
# Google falls back to re-checking the other verification tokens it
# knows about, finds this one redirecting, and unverifies the property.
# The property disappearing is how you find out, and by then the
# sitemap submissions and the URL inspection history have gone with it.
#
# A 200 rewrite rather than a redirect: the same content served at the
# `.html` URL, no hop, which is exactly what the verifier checks for.
# It is a single exact path, so it shadows nothing — see the long note
# above on why a catch-all cannot come back.
/google0dbb222546d95f4f.html  /google0dbb222546d95f4f  200
